$) satisfy the following addition problem $$ \\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} & & & & A & B_7\\\\ &+ & & & B & A_7\\\\ \\cline{2-6} & & & A & A & 0_7\\\\ \\end{array} $$what is the product of the digits represented by $A$ and $B$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b27ed2962c8496890ff3a38d18f766132088acafb8a3fbf31d8ce2d920231128"},"seconds":36.643487215042114,"seed":1107774921720517575,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to understand the addition in base 7. The given problem is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}\n& & & & A & B_7\\\\\n&+ & & & B & A_7\\\\\n\\cline{2-6}\n& & & A & A & 0_7\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThis means we need to add the numbers $AB_7$ and $BA_7$ in base 7, and the result should be $AA0_7$. Let's convert these numbers to base 10 to make the addition clearer.\n\nThe number $AB_7$ in base 10 is $7A + B$.\nThe number $BA_7$ in base 10 is $7B + A$.\nThe number $AA0_7$ in base 10 is $49A + 7A = 56A$.\n\nSo, we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[\n(7A + B) + (7B + A) = 56A\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\n8A + 8B = 56A\n\\]\n\nSubtracting $8A$ from both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n8B = 48A\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 8, we get:\n\n\\[\nB = 6A\n\\]\n\nSince $A$ and $B$ are digits in base 7, they must be between 0 and 6. The only possible value for $A$ that makes $B$ a digit in base 7 is $A = 1$, which gives $B = 6$. Let's check if this solution works:\n\nIf $A = 1$ and $B = 6$, then $AB_7 = 16_7 = 7 \\cdot 1 + 6 = 13$ in base 10.\nAnd $BA_7 = 61_7 = 7 \\cdot 6 + 1 = 43$ in base 10.\nAdding these, we get $13 + 43 = 56$, which is $AA0_7 = 110_7 = 7 \\cdot 7 + 7 \\cdot 1 = 49 + 7 = 56$ in base 10.\n\nSo, the solution $A = 1$ and $B = 6$ is correct. The product of the digits represented by $A$ and $B$ is:\n\n\\[\n1 \\times 6 = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{6}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
